tion (2): 2a\u00b2b = -2. Let's solve for b.\n\nFirst, compute a\u00b2. Since \u03c9\u00b2 = [(-1 + i\u221a3)/2]^2 = (1 - 2i\u221a3 - 3)/4 = (-2 - 2i\u221a3)/4 = (-1 - i\u221a3)/2 = \u03c9\u00b2. Wait, but \u03c9\u00b2 is another cube root, so a\u00b2 = \u03c9\u00b2. Therefore, equation (2):\n\n2 * \u03c9\u00b2 * b = -2 => b = -2 / (2\u03c9\u00b2) = -1 / \u03c9\u00b2. But \u03c9\u00b3 = 1, so 1/\u03c9\u00b2 = \u03c9. Thus, b = -\u03c9.\n\nSo, b = -\u03c9 = -(-1 + i\u221a3)/2 = (1 - i\u221a3)/2.\n\nNow, move to equation (3): a(2ac + b\u00b2) + ab = 4.\n\nWe have a = \u03c9, b = -\u03c9, and need to find c.\n\nFirst, compute 2ac: 2\u03c9 c.\n\nThen compute b\u00b2: (-\u03c9)\u00b2 = \u03c9\u00b2.\n\nThus, equation (3):\n\n\u03c9*(2\u03c9 c + \u03c9\u00b2) + \u03c9*(-\u03c9) = 4.\n\nSimplify each term:\n\nFirst term: \u03c9*(2\u03c9 c + \u03c9\u00b2) = 2\u03c9\u00b2 c + \u03c9\u00b3.\n\nBut \u03c9\u00b3 = 1, so this becomes 2\u03c9\u00b2 c + 1.\n\nSecond term: \u03c9*(-\u03c9) = -\u03c9\u00b2.\n\nAdding them together:\n\n2\u03c9\u00b2 c + 1 - \u03c9\u00b2 = 4.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n2\u03c9\u00b2 c + (1 - \u03c9\u00b2) = 4.\n\nLet me solve for c:\n\n2\u03c9\u00b2 c = 4 - (1 - \u03c9\u00b2) = 3 + \u03c9\u00b2.\n\nThus, c = (3 + \u03c9\u00b2)/(2\u03c9\u00b2).\n\nBut we can express \u03c9\u00b2 in terms of \u03c9. Since \u03c9\u00b2 = (-1 - i\u221a3)/2. Let's compute numerator and denominator.\n\nNumerator: 3 + \u03c9\u00b2 = 3 + (-1 - i\u221a3)/2 = (6 -1 -i\u221a3)/2 = (5 - i\u221a3)/2.\n\nDenominator: 2\u03c9\u00b2 = 2*(-1 - i\u221a3)/2 = -1 - i\u221a3.\n\nSo, c = (5 - i\u221a3)/2 divided by (-1 - i\u221a3).\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator to rationalize:\n\nc = [(5 - i\u221a3)/2] * [(-1 + i\u221a3)/(-1 + i\u221a3)] / [(-1 - i\u221a3)(-1 + i\u221a3)]\n\nFirst compute denominator of the division:\n\n(-1)^2 - (i\u221a3)^2 = 1 - (-3) = 4.\n\nSo, denominator is 4.\n\nNumerator:\n\n(5 - i\u221a3)(-1 + i\u221a3) = 5*(-1) + 5*(i\u22