 a\u00b2 + b\u00b2\n\nThus, t = (c\u00b2 + a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) / (c\u00b2 + 2c a + a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nTherefore, point E has coordinates t(c + a, b) = [ (c\u00b2 + a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/(c\u00b2 + 2c a + a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) ]*(c + a, b)\n\nTherefore, AE is the distance from A to E, which is t times the length of AC. The length of AC is sqrt( (c + a)^2 + b\u00b2 ), so AE = t sqrt( (c + a)^2 + b\u00b2 )\n\nAC * AE = sqrt( (c + a)^2 + b\u00b2 ) * [ (c\u00b2 + a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/( (c + a)^2 + b\u00b2 ) ) * sqrt( (c + a)^2 + b\u00b2 ) ] = (c\u00b2 + a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )\n\nBut AB\u00b2 + AD\u00b2 = c\u00b2 + (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ). Hence, AB\u00b2 + AD\u00b2 = AC * AE. Therefore, proved.\n\nSince this coordinate approach works, and the problem doesn't specify the method, this is a valid proof. However, the problem might be expecting a synthetic geometric proof. Let me try to find another way.\n\nAlternative approach using\u76f8\u4f3c triangles (similar triangles):\n\nLet\u2019s consider triangles formed by the points. Since ABCD is a parallelogram, AB is congruent to CD, and AD congruent to BC. Diagonals bisect each other. The circle passes through A, B, D and intersects AC at E. \n\nLet\u2019s consider the power of point E with respect to the circle. Since E is on the circle, its power is zero. But E is also on AC, so maybe use the power of point E with respect to another circle, but not sure.\n\nWait, another idea: Use the theorem of intersecting chords. If two chords intersect at a point, then the products of the segments are equal. In this case, chords BD and AC intersect at O, the midpoint. Therefore, AO * OC = BO * OD. But since O is the midpoint, AO = OC and BO = OD, so AO\u00b2 = BO\u00b2. Therefore, AO = BO, which would imply the parallelogram is a rectangle. But this is only true in a rectangle. Therefore, this approach doesn't help.\n\nAlternatively, since E is on the circle ABD, we can use the cyclic quadrilateral properties. For example, in cyclic quadrilateral A