riangles. Therefore:\n\n(1/2)*a*b*sin(theta) = r*(a + b + e)/2\n\nSimilarly,\n\n(1/2)*b*c*sin(phi) = r*(b + c + e)/2\n\nAnd the same for the other two triangles.\n\nThis gives:\n\na*b*sin(theta) = r*(a + b + e)\n\nb*c*sin(phi) = r*(b + c + e)\n\nc*d*sin(psi) = r*(c + d + e)\n\nd*a*sin(omega) = r*(d + a + e)\n\nWhere theta, phi, psi, omega are the angles at the vertices B, C, D, A in triangles ABC, BCD, CDA, DAB respectively.\n\nHmm, this seems like a system of equations. Maybe if I can relate these equations to each other.\n\nAlso, note that in the quadrilateral ABCD, the sum of angles at each vertex is 180 degrees? Wait, no, in a convex quadrilateral, the sum of all four internal angles is 360 degrees, but each individual angle is less than 180 degrees. However, the angles theta, phi, psi, omega mentioned here are the angles within the triangles, which are not necessarily the same as the internal angles of the quadrilateral. For example, angle theta in triangle ABC is the angle at vertex B between sides AB and BC, which is the same as the internal angle of the quadrilateral at B. Similarly for the others. Wait, actually, in the triangles ABC, BCD, CDA, DAB, the angles at the vertices of the quadrilateral are the same as the internal angles of the quadrilateral. The other angles in the triangles are at the intersection points with the diagonals.\n\nWait, no. For triangle ABC, the vertices are A, B, C. The angle at B in triangle ABC is the same as the internal angle of the quadrilateral at B. Similarly, in triangle BCD, the angle at C is the same as the internal angle of the quadrilateral at C, and so on. Therefore, theta, phi, psi, omega correspond to the internal angles at B, C, D, A of the quadrilateral.\n\nBut in the quadrilateral, the sum of the internal angles is 360 degrees. So theta + phi + psi + omega = 360 degrees.\n\nBut each of these angles is involved in one of the triangles. So, using the above equations:\n\na*b*sin(theta) = r*(a + b + e)\n\nb*c*sin(phi