b + c + e). Similarly for DAB:\n\nr = (a h)/(d + a + e) = (a h)/(c + a + e)\n\nBut in the isosceles trapezoid, AD = BC = c, so DA = c. Therefore, the inradii for triangles CDA and BCD are both (b h)/(b + c + e), and for triangles ABC and DAB are (a h)/(a + c + e).\n\nFor all four inradii to be equal, we must have:\n\n(a h)/(a + c + e) = (b h)/(b + c + e)\n\nCancel h:\n\na / (a + c + e) = b / (b + c + e)\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\na(b + c + e) = b(a + c + e)\n\nExpand:\n\nab + a c + a e = ab + b c + b e\n\nCancel ab:\n\na c + a e = b c + b e\n\nFactor:\n\na(c + e) = b(c + e)\n\nAssuming c + e \u2260 0 (which they aren't), we have a = b\n\nTherefore, in the isosceles trapezoid, the inradii are equal only if a = b, meaning the trapezoid is actually a rectangle (since the bases are equal). Therefore, in this case, the only isosceles trapezoid with equal inradii is a rectangle.\n\nTherefore, even if we supposed ABCD was an isosceles trapezoid, it must be a rectangle to satisfy the inradius condition.\n\nHence, combining this with the earlier result that a = c and b = d (making it a parallelogram) along with equal diagonals, we conclude that ABCD must be a rectangle.\n\nTherefore, the proof is complete.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that a convex quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) with equal inradii in all four triangles \\(ABC\\), \\(BCD\\), \\(CDA\\), and \\(DAB\\) must be a rectangle, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Equal Diagonals**:  \n   The inradius \\(r\\) of a triangle is given by \\(r = \\frac{\\text{Area}}{\\text{semiperimeter}}\\). Since all four triangles have equal inradii, their areas are proportional to their semiperimeters. By equating the total areas of triangles \\(ABC + CDA\\) and \\(BCD + DAB\\), we derive that the diagonals \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\) must be equal (\\(AC = BD\\)).\n\n2. **Opposite Sides Equal**:  \n   Using the inradius condition for triangles \\(ABC\\) and \\(BCD\\), we equate their area-to-semiperimeter ratios:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a \\cdot b \\cdot \