ake real and imaginary parts, then we get sine and cosine functions. So, the general solution might be a linear combination of functions like cos(12\u03c0n x + \u03c6) and cos(14\u03c0m x + \u03b8), etc. But since the equation is linear, the solutions are superpositions of such functions. However, the problem states that f is bounded (|f(x)| \u22641), which is consistent with these trigonometric functions since they are bounded.\n\nBut we need to show that f is periodic. So, each of these exponentials has a period. For example, e^{12\u03c0i n x} has period 1/(6n), and e^{14\u03c0i m x} has period 1/(7m). Therefore, any linear combination of such functions would have a common period, which would be the least common multiple of their individual periods. So, if the solution is built from terms with periods 1/(6n) and 1/(7m), then the common period would be the LCM of 1/6 and 1/7, which is 1, since LCM of 6 and 7 is 42, so LCM of 1/6 and 1/7 is 1/ (GCD(6,7)) )=1/1=1. Wait, actually LCM of fractions is a bit different. The LCM of 1/6 and 1/7 is LCM(1,1)/GCD(6,7) =1/1=1. Hmm, so the period would be 1? But maybe not necessarily. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the individual periods for e^{12\u03c0i n x} is 1/(6n), and for e^{14\u03c0i m x} is 1/(7m). The LCM of these periods would be 1/(GCD(6n,7m)). Hmm, since LCM of 1/a and 1/b is 1/GCD(a,b). So if the exponents are 12\u03c0n and 14\u03c0m, then the frequencies are 6n and 7m. So the periods are 1/(6n) and 1/(7m). The LCM of these periods would be LCM(1/(6n), 1/(7m)) = 1 / GCD(6n,7m). Since 6 and 7 are coprime, GCD(6n,7m) = GCD(n,m). Therefore, the LCM period would be 1 / GCD(n,m). But since n and m are integers, unless restricted, GCD(n,m) can be 1. So the period could be 1. Hmm, but this might depend on the specific solution. \n\nHowever, since the problem states that f is bounded and the functional equation is linear, the general solution is a linear combination of such exponentials (or sines and cosines). Therefore, any such combination would be periodic if all the individual functions have commensurate periods (i.e., their periods have a common multiple). Since the original functional equation is linear and homogeneous, the solutions form a vector space, and the periodic solutions would be those with frequencies that are integer multiples of some base frequency.\n\nBut perhaps there's a smarter approach. Let me think again about the functional equation:\n\nf(x + 13/42) + f(x) = f(x + 1/6) + f(x + 1/7).\n\nLet me try to iterate this equation. Maybe shift x by some amount and apply the equation again. For example, let me substitute x with x + 1/6:\n\nf(x + 1/6 + 13/42) + f(x + 1/6) = f(x + 1/6 + 1/6) + f(x + 1/6 + 1/7).\n\nSimplify the terms:\n\n13/42 + 1/6 = 13/42 + 7/42 = 20/42 = 10/21 \u2248 0.476...\n\nSimilarly, 1/6 + 1/6 = 2/6 = 1/3, and 1/6 + 1/7 = (7 + 6)/42 = 13/42. So the equation becomes:\n\nf(x + 10/21) + f(x + 1/6) = f(x + 1/3) + f(x + 13/42).\n\nBut the original equation is f(x +13/42) + f(x) = f(x +1/6) + f(x +1/7). Maybe combining these two equations can lead us somewhere. Let me write both:\n\n1. f(x +13/42) + f(x) = f(x +1/6) + f(x +1/7). (Original)\n\n2. f(x +10/21) + f(x +1/6) = f(x +1/3) + f(x +13/42). (After substituting x \u2192 x +1/6)\n\nIf I subtract equation 1 from equation 2, we get:\n\n[f(x +10/21) + f(x +1/6)] - [f(x +13/42) + f(x)] = [f(x +1/3) + f(x +13/42)] - [f(x +1/6) + f(x +1/7)].\n\nSimplify left side: f(x +10/21) - f(x +13/42) + f(x +1/6) - f(x).\n\nRight side: f(x +1/3) - f(x +1/6) + f(x +13/42) - f(x +1/7).\n\nHmm, this seems complicated. Maybe instead, let's try to express f(x +13/42) from equation 1:\n\nf(x +13/42) = f(x +1/6) + f(x +1/7) - f(x).\n\nThen plug this into equation 2:\n\nf(x +10/21) + f(x +1/6) = f(x +1/3) + [f(x +1/6) + f(x +1/7) - f(x)].\n\nSimplify the right side: f(x +1/3) + f(x +1/6) + f(x +1/7) - f(x).\n\nSo equation 2 becomes:\n\nf(x +10/21) + f(x +1/6) = f(x +1/3) + f(x +1/6) + f(x +1/7) - f(x).\n\nCancel f(x +1/6) from 