ain 90 degrees. Then, when constructing the parallelepiped with edges PA, PB, PC, the point Q is the sum of these vectors from P. So Q is located at P plus PA plus PB plus PC. Therefore, the coordinates of Q would be P + PA + PB + PC. But since PA, PB, PC are vectors from P, their sum would be a vector from P to Q.\n\nBut since A, B, C are on the sphere, maybe there's a relation between the lengths of PA, PB, PC and the radius of the sphere. Let me think. Suppose the sphere has radius R and center O. Let\u2019s denote the position vectors of points A, B, C with respect to O as vectors a, b, c. Then, since A, B, C are on the sphere, their magnitudes are R: |a| = |b| = |c| = R.\n\nPoint P is fixed inside the sphere, so let\u2019s denote its position vector as p. Then, vectors PA, PB, PC are vectors from P to A, B, C, which are a - p, b - p, c - p respectively. The condition given is that the angles between PA, PB, PC are 90 degrees. So, the dot products between each pair of these vectors should be zero:\n\n(a - p) \u00b7 (b - p) = 0\n\n(a - p) \u00b7 (c - p) = 0\n\n(b - p) \u00b7 (c - p) = 0\n\nSo these are the orthogonality conditions. Then, the parallelepiped formed by PA, PB, PC as edges has vertex Q opposite to P. The coordinates of Q would be P + PA + PB + PC. Wait, no. If the parallelepiped is formed by vectors PA, PB, PC emanating from P, then the other vertices are P + PA, P + PB, P + PC, and then Q would be P + PA + PB + PC. So yes, Q is located at P plus the sum of the vectors PA, PB, PC.\n\nBut PA is A - P, so Q = P + (A - P) + (B - P) + (C - P) = A + B + C - 2P. Therefore, Q is located at the point A + B + C - 2P. But this is assuming vector addition with respect to the origin. Wait, if we are working in coordinates, then yes. Let me confirm.\n\nIf P is a point with coordinates (p_x, p_y, p_z), and A, B, C are points with coordinates (a_x, a_y, a_z), etc., then PA is the vector from P to A: (a_x - p_x, a_y - p_y, a_z - p_z). Similarly for PB and PC. Then, the parallelepiped with edges PA, PB, PC would have vertices at P, P + PA, P + PB, P + PC, P + PA + PB, etc., and the opposite vertex Q would be P + PA + PB + PC. Therefore, the coordinates of Q are:\n\n(p_x + (a_x - p_x) + (b_x - p_x) + (c_x - p_x),\n\np_y + (a_y - p_y) + (b_y - p_y) + (c_y - p_y),\n\np_z + (a_z - p_z) + (b_z - p_z) + (c_z - p_z))\n\nSimplifying each coordinate:\n\nFor x-coordinate: p_x + (a_x - p_x) + (b_x - p_x) + (c_x - p_x) = a_x + b_x + c_x - 2p_x\n\nSimilarly for y and z. Therefore, Q has coordinates (a_x + b_x + c_x - 2p_x, a_y + b_y + c_y - 2p_y, a_z + b_z + c_z - 2p_z). So Q = A + B + C - 2P. So in vector terms, Q = a + b + c - 2p, where a, b, c are the position vectors of A, B, C with respect to the origin, and p is the position vector of P.\n\nSo the problem reduces to finding the locus of Q = A + B + C - 2P as A, B, C move on the sphere's surface with the orthogonality conditions on (A - P), (B - P), (C - P).\n\nGiven that, maybe we can express the position vectors a, b, c in terms of p and some orthogonal vectors.\n\nLet me denote u = a - p, v = b - p, w = c - p. Then, since u, v, w are mutually orthogonal, they form an orthogonal set of vectors. Also, since A, B, C lie on the sphere of radius R centered at the origin, we have |a| = |b| = |c| = R.\n\nExpressed in terms of u, v, w:\n\na = p + u\n\nb = p + v\n\nc = p + w\n\nSo Q = a + b + c - 2p = (p + u) + (p + v) + (p + w) - 2p = p + u + v + w.\n\nSo Q = p + u + v + w.\n\nBut u, v, w are mutually orthogonal vectors. Let me consider the properties of u, v, w.\n\nSince u, v, w are orthogonal, the squared length of Q - p is |u + v + w|\u00b2 = |u|\u00b2 + |v|\u00b2 + |w|\u00b2, because the dot products between u, v, w are zero.\n\nSo |Q - p|\u00b2 = |u|\u00b2 + |v|\u00b2 + |w|\u00b2.\n\nBut also, since a = p + u is on the sphere, |a|\u00b2 = |p + u|\u00b2 = R\u00b2.\n\nSimilarly, |p + v|\u00b2 = R\u00b2 and |p + w|\u00b2 = R\u00b2.\n\nExpanding |p + u|\u00b2 = |p|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 u + |u|\u00b2 = R\u00b2.\n\nSimilarly for v and w.\n\nSo, for each of u, v, w, we have:\n\n|p|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 u + |u|\u00b2 = R\u00b2\n\n|p|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 v + |v|\u00b2 = R\u00b2\n\n|p|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 w + |w|\u00b2 = R\u00b2\n\nLet me denote |p| as the magnitude of vector p, let's call it d (since P is fixed inside the sphere, d is a constant less than R). Then, for each vector u, v, w:\n\nd\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 u + |u|\u00b2 = R\u00b2\n\nSimilarly for v and w.\n\nSo, rearranged:\n\n|u|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 u = R\u00b2 - d\u00b2\n\nSame for |v|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 v and |w|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 w.\n\nLet me note that Q - p = u + v + w. So |Q - p|\u00b2 = |u|\u00b2 + |v|\u00b2 + |w|\u00b2, since u, v, w are orthogonal.\n\nSo, if I can find |u|\u00b2 + |v|\u00b2 + |w|\u00b2, that will give me |Q - p|\u00b2. Let's compute this sum.\n\nFrom the three equations:\n\n|u|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 u = R\u00b2 - d\u00b2\n\n|v|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 v = R\u00b2 - d\u00b2\n\n|w|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 w = R\u00b2 - d\u00b2\n\nAdding them together:\n\n(|u|\u00b2 + |v|\u00b2 + |w|\u00b2) + 2p \u00b7 (u + v + w) = 3(R\u00b2 - d\u00b2)\n\nBut u + v + w = Q - p. So:\n\n(|u|\u00b2 + |v|\u00b2 + |w|\u00b2) + 2p \u00b7 (Q - p) = 3(R\u00b2 - d\u00b2)\n\nBut |u|\u00b2 + |v|\u00b2 + |w|\u00b2 = |Q - p|\u00b2. Therefore:\n\n|Q - p|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 (Q - p) = 3(R\u00b2 - d\u00b2)\n\nLet me expand the left side:\n\n|Q - p|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 Q - 2|p|\u00b2 = 3(R\u00b2 - d\u00b2)\n\nBut |Q - p|\u00b2 = |Q|\u00b2 - 2Q \u00b7 p + |p|\u00b2. Substituting that in:\n\n(|Q|\u00b2 - 2Q \u00b7 p + |p|\u00b2) + 2p \u00b7 Q - 2|p|\u00b2 = 3(R\u00b2 - d\u00b2)\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n|Q|\u00b2 - 2Q \u00b7 p + |p|\u00b2 + 2p \u00b7 Q - 2|p|\u00b2 = |Q|\u00b2 - |p|\u00b2\n\nTherefore:\n\n|Q|\u00b2 - |p|\u00b2 = 3(R\u00b2 - d\u00b2)\n\nSo:\n\n|Q|\u00b2 = 3(R\u00b2 - d\u00b2) + |p|\u00b2\n\nBut |p|\u00b2 = d\u00b2, so:\n\n|Q|\u00b2 = 3R\u00b2 - 3d\u00b2 + d\u00b2 = 3R\u00b2 - 2d\u00b2\n\nTherefore, |Q|\u00b2 = 3R\u00b2 - 2d\