e groups consists of knights and liars. Let\u2019s define:\n\n- In group A: Let k_A be the number of knights, and l_A be the number of liars. So k_A + l_A = A.\n- In group B: Let k_B be the number of knights, and l_B be the number of liars. So k_B + l_B = B.\n\nKnights always tell the truth, so:\n\n- k_A knights in group A say K is even \u21d2 if k_A > 0, then K is even.\n- l_A liars in group A say K is even \u21d2 lie \u21d2 K is odd.\n- k_B knights in group B say L is odd \u21d2 since L = 2021 - K, this is equivalent to K is even \u21d2 if k_B > 0, then K is even.\n- l_B liars in group B say L is odd \u21d2 lie \u21d2 L is even \u21d2 K is odd.\n\nTherefore, the existence of knights in either group A or B requires K to be even. The existence of liars in either group requires K to be odd. But K cannot be both even and odd. Therefore, the only way this can happen is if one of the groups (A or B) has no knights and the other has no liars. Let's explore this.\n\nSuppose K is even. Then:\n\n- All knights (K) must be in groups A and B where they tell the truth. Therefore, k_A and k_B must be such that k_A + k_B = K.\n- All liars (L = 2021 - K) must be in groups A and B where they lie. So l_A + l_B = L.\n\nBut in group A, liars say K is even (which is true), which they cannot do. Therefore, l_A must be 0. Similarly, in group B, liars say L is odd (which is true, since K is even \u21d2 L is odd), which they cannot do. Therefore, l_B must be 0. Therefore, all liars must not exist, which contradicts L = 2021 - K \u2265 1 (since K \u2264 2020).\n\nTherefore, K cannot be even.\n\nNow suppose K is odd. Then:\n\n- All knights (K) must be in groups A and B where they tell the truth. But:\n\n  - In group A, knights say K is even, which is false \u21d2 knights cannot be in group A \u21d2 k_A = 0.\n  - In group B, knights say L is odd. But K is odd \u21d2 L = 2021 - K = even \u21d2 knights in group B would be lying \u21d2 impossible \u21d2 k_B = 0.\n\nTherefore, all knights must be