binary:\n\n1: 0001\n\n2: 0010\n\n3: 0011\n\n4: 0100\n\n5: 0101\n\n6: 0110\n\n7: 0111\n\n8: 1000\n\n9: 1001\n\n10:1010\n\n11:1011\n\n12:1100\n\n13:1101\n\n14:1110\n\n15:1111\n\nFor each bit position:\n\nBit 1 (LSB): 1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15\n\nBit 2: 2,3,6,7,10,11,14,15\n\nBit 3:4,5,6,7,12,13,14,15\n\nBit 4:8,9,10,11,12,13,14,15\n\nSo if box 7 is missing:\n\nWeighing 1: missing 1 unit (bit 1 set) \u2192 deficit\n\nWeighing 2: missing 1 unit (bit 2 set) \u2192 deficit\n\nWeighing 3: missing 1 unit (bit 3 set) \u2192 deficit\n\nWeighing 4: no deficit (bit 4 not set)\n\nWait, no. Box 7 is in group for bits 1,2,3. So if box 7 is missing, each weighing that includes it will show a deficit. Therefore:\n\nWeighing 1: 800 -1 =799 \u2192 deficit\n\nWeighing 2: 800 -1=799 \u2192 deficit\n\nWeighing 3: 800 -1=799 \u2192 deficit\n\nWeighing 4: 800 \u2192 no deficit\n\nSo the binary result is 0111, which is 7. Correct.\n\nSimilarly, if box 15 is missing:\n\nAll weighings show deficit: 1111, which is 15.\n\nThus, this method works and requires 4 weighings.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 4 weighings using a binary encoding approach.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine which box is missing a product with fewer than 14 weighings, we can use a **binary encoding method**, which reduces the number of weighings to **4**. Here's the systematic approach:\n\n### Key Insight:\nEach box is assigned a unique 4-bit binary identifier (since \\(15 = 1111_2\\), requiring 4 bits). By strategically weighing groups of boxes based on these bits, the missing box can be identified through the pattern of weight deficits.\n\n### Procedure:\n1. **Label Boxes with Binary Numbers**: Assign each of the 15 boxes a unique 4-bit binary number (e.g., Box 1 = \\(0001_2\\), Box 2 = \\(0010_2\\), ..., Box 15 = \\(1111_2\\)).\n\n2. **Weigh Groups Based on Bit Positions**:\n   - **Weighing 1 (Bit 1 - Least Significant Bit)**: Weigh all boxes where the first bit is \\(1\\) (Boxes 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15).  \n     - Expected 