n3. **Group by Binary Digits**\n   - Create groups based on each binary digit position where the digit is \u20181\u2019:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{c|c}\n     \\text{Group} & \\text{Boxes} \\\\\n     \\hline\n     \\text{1st Digit} & 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15 \\\\\n     \\text{2nd Digit} & 2, 3, 6, 7, 10, 11, 14, 15 \\\\\n     \\text{3rd Digit} & 4, 5, 6, 7, 12, 13, 14, 15 \\\\\n     \\text{4th Digit} & 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 \\\\\n     \\end{array}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Weigh Box Groups**\n   - Weigh the boxes in each group separately. Note the groups where the total weight is less than expected:\n     - If the weight is normal, record a 0.\n     - If the weight is lighter, record a 1.\n\n5. **Decode the Error Box**\n   - Combine the recorded digits (from step 4) into a single four-digit binary number:\n     - Example if second and fourth group are lighter: results in a binary number \\(0101_2\\).\n\n6. **Determining the Faulty Box**\n   - Convert this binary number into a decimal number to identify the box.\n     - \\(0101_2\\) results in box number \\(5\\).\n\n### Conclusion:\nBy weighing the groups of boxes as described and interpreting the results in binary, we can determine the faulty box with just 4 weighings instead of 14. Therefore, it is indeed possible to identify the box which was not packed correctly by this streamlined method.\n\n$\\boxed{4}$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The base of the pyramid is an equilateral triangle with a side length of 6. One of the lateral edges is perpendicular to the plane of the base and has a length of 4. Find the radius of the sphere circumscribed around the pyramid.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let's see. I need to find the radius of the circumscribed sphere around this pyramid. The base is an equilateral triangle with side length 6, and one of the lateral edges is perpendicular to the base with length 4. Hmm. Okay, so first, let me visualize the pyramid. The base is an equilateral triangle, so all sides are 6, and all angles are 60 degrees. Now, a lateral edge is perpendicular to the base. Wait, lateral edge\u2014so in a pyramid, the lateral edges are the edges from the apex to the base vertices. So one of these edges is perpendicular to the base. That must mean that the apex is directly above one of the base vertices, right? Because if the lateral edge is perpendicular, then the apex is along the line perpendicular to the base at that vertex. So, the apex is 4 units above that vertex.\n\nLet me confirm: the lateral edge is perpendicular to the base's plane and has length 4. So, if the base is in, say, the xy-plane, then the apex would be at (x, y, 4) where (x, y, 0) is one of the vertices. But since it's perpendicular, the apex is directly above that vertex. So, if we take the base triangle, let's label the vertices. Let's set up coordinates to model this.\n\nLet me assign coordinates to the base. Let's choose vertex A at (0, 0, 0). Since it's an equilateral triangle with side length 6, the coordinates can be placed as follows. Let me recall that the height (altitude) of an equilateral triangle with side length a is (\u221a3/2)a. So, the altitude here is (\u221a3/2)*6 = 3\u221a3. So, if vertex A is at (0, 0, 0), vertex B can be at (6, 0, 0), and vertex C at (3, 3\u221a3, 0). That centers the triangle with base AB along the x-axis and point C above the midpoint of AB.\n\nNow, the apex D is connected to one of the vertices via a lateral edge perpendicular to the base. Let's say it's connected to vertex A. Then, since the edge AD is perpendicular to the base, which is in the xy-plane, the coordinates of D would be (0, 0, 4). Wait, but the length of AD is 4. So, if A is at (0, 0, 0), then D is at (0, 0, 4). That makes AD of length 4, which is perpendicular to the base. So, the pyramid has vertices A(0,0,0), B(6,0,0), C(3, 3\u221a3,0), and D(0,0,4).\n\nBut wait, in a pyramid, the apex is connected to all