 = 0 \u2192 -8l = -16 \u2192 l = 2\n\nSo, l = 2. The z-coordinate of the center is 2.\n\nNow, we have h = 3 and l = 2. Substitute these into equation 1 and equation 3 to find k.\n\nFrom equation 1: (3)\u00b2 + k\u00b2 + (2)\u00b2 = R\u00b2 \u2192 9 + k\u00b2 +4 = R\u00b2 \u2192 13 + k\u00b2 = R\u00b2.\n\nFrom equation 3: (3 - 3)\u00b2 + (k - 3\u221a3)\u00b2 + (2)\u00b2 = R\u00b2 \u2192 0 + (k - 3\u221a3)\u00b2 +4 = R\u00b2 \u2192 (k - 3\u221a3)\u00b2 +4 = R\u00b2.\n\nSo, set equations 1 and 3 equal:\n\n13 + k\u00b2 = (k - 3\u221a3)\u00b2 +4\n\nExpand the right-hand side:\n\n(k\u00b2 -6\u221a3 k + 27) +4 = k\u00b2 -6\u221a3 k +31\n\nSo:\n\n13 + k\u00b2 = k\u00b2 -6\u221a3 k +31\n\nSubtract k\u00b2 from both sides:\n\n13 = -6\u221a3 k +31\n\nSubtract 31:\n\n-18 = -6\u221a3 k\n\nDivide both sides by -6\u221a3:\n\n(-18)/(-6\u221a3) = k \u2192 3/\u221a3 = k \u2192 \u221a3 = k. Wait:\n\nWait, 18 divided by 6 is 3, so 3/\u221a3. Rationalizing the denominator: 3/\u221a3 = \u221a3. So, k = \u221a3.\n\nTherefore, the center of the sphere is at (3, \u221a3, 2). Now, compute R\u00b2 from equation 1:\n\nR\u00b2 = 3\u00b2 + (\u221a3)\u00b2 + 2\u00b2 = 9 + 3 +4 = 16. Therefore, R = 4.\n\nWait, that's interesting. The radius is 4? Let me verify this with another point, say point D.\n\nCompute the distance from center (3, \u221a3, 2) to D(0,0,4):\n\n\u221a[(3 -0)^2 + (\u221a3 -0)^2 + (2 -4)^2] = \u221a[9 + 3 + (-2)^2] = \u221a[12 +4] = \u221a16 =4. That works.\n\nCheck point B(6,0,0):\n\nDistance from (3, \u221a3, 2) to (6,0,0):\n\n\u221a[(6-3)^2 + (0 - \u221a3)^2 + (0 -2)^2] = \u221a[9 + 3 +4] = \u221a16 =4. Correct.\n\nCheck point C(3, 3\u221a3,0):\n\nDistance from (3, \u221a3, 2) to (3, 3\u221a3,0):\n\n\u221a[(3-3)^2 + (3\u221a3 - \u221a3)^2 + (0 -2)^2] = \u221a[0 + (2\u221a3)^2 +4] = \u221a[12 +4] = \u221a16 =4. Correct.\n\nAnd point A(0,0,0):\n\nDistance from (3, \u221a3, 2) to (0,0,0):\n\n\u221a[9 +3 +4] = \u221a16 =4. Correct. So all points are at distance 4 from the center (