sociative, we can use the concept of Catalan numbers, which count the number of ways to fully parenthesize a product.\n\n1. **Identify the number of multiplications:**\n\n   Given five objects \\(a, b, c, d, e\\), there are \\(4\\) multiplications needed to compute the product \\(a \\cdot b \\cdot c \\cdot d \\cdot e\\).\n\n   We denote the \\(n\\)-th Catalan number by \\(C_n\\). The number of different ways to fully parenthesize a sequence of \\(n + 1\\) factors (e.g., \\(a \\cdot b \\cdot c \\cdots \\cdot z\\)) is given by the Catalan number \\(C_n\\).\n\n   Here, we have \\(5\\) factors which corresponds to \\(n = 4\\).\n\n2. **Calculate the 4th Catalan number:**\n\n   The \\(n\\)-th Catalan number is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   C_n = \\frac{1}{n + 1} \\binom{2n}{n}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n = 4\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   C_4 = \\frac{1}{4 + 1} \\binom{8}{4} = \\frac{1}{5} \\binom{8}{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Compute \\(\\binom{8}{4}\\):**\n\n   The binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{8}{4}\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{8}{4} = \\frac{8!}{4! \\cdot 4!} = \\frac{8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5}{4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = 70\n   \\]\n\n4. **Finalize the calculation for \\(C_4\\):**\n\n   Substitute \\( \\binom{8}{4} = 70 \\) into the formula for \\(C_4\\):\n   \\[\n   C_4 = \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 70 = 14\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to interpret the product \\(a \\cdot b \\cdot c \\cdot d \\cdot e\\) is \\(14\\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nFor a certain polynomial \\( P(x) \\) of degree \\( n \\) and certain numbers \\( a<b \\), the following inequalities hold:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(a) & < 0, && -P'(a) \\leq 0, && P''(a) \\leq 0, && \\ldots, && (-1)^n P^{(n)}(a) \\leq 0, \\\\\nP(b) & > 0, && P'(b) \\geq 0, && P''(b) \\geq 0, && \\ldots, && P^{(n)}(b) \\geq 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nProve that all real roots of the polynomial \\( P(x) 