equation.\n\nWait, let's go back to the expression for x\u00b2:\n\nx\u00b2 = (a\u00b2 - e\u00b2) / [c\u00b2(1/e\u00b2 - 1/a\u00b2)]\n\nBut let's compute 1/e\u00b2 - 1/a\u00b2:\n\n1/e\u00b2 - 1/a\u00b2 = (a\u00b2 - e\u00b2)/(a\u00b2e\u00b2)\n\nTherefore, substituting back:\n\nx\u00b2 = (a\u00b2 - e\u00b2) / [c\u00b2 * (a\u00b2 - e\u00b2)/(a\u00b2e\u00b2)] ) = (a\u00b2 - e\u00b2) * (a\u00b2e\u00b2)/(c\u00b2(a\u00b2 - e\u00b2)) ) = a\u00b2e\u00b2 / c\u00b2\n\nTherefore, x\u00b2 = (a\u00b2e\u00b2)/c\u00b2 => x = \u00b1(ae)/c\n\nSo, the x-coordinate of the common points is \u00b1(ae)/c. Interesting. Now, to find the distances d1 and d2 from point P(x, y) to the foci F1(-c, 0) and F2(c, 0).\n\nSince the point P is (ae/c, y) or (-ae/c, y). Let's take the positive x case first.\n\nCompute d1 = distance from (ae/c, y) to (-c, 0):\n\nd1 = sqrt[(ae/c + c)^2 + y\u00b2]\n\nSimilarly, d2 = sqrt[(ae/c - c)^2 + y\u00b2]\n\nBut we also know from the ellipse that d1 + d2 = 2a, and from the hyperbola |d1 - d2| = 2e.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute d1 and d2 using the coordinates.\n\nFirst, let's compute y\u00b2 from either the ellipse or hyperbola equation. Let's use the ellipse:\n\ny\u00b2 = b\u00b2(1 - x\u00b2/a\u00b2) = (a\u00b2 - c\u00b2)(1 - (a\u00b2e\u00b2)/(c\u00b2a\u00b2)) = (a\u00b2 - c\u00b2)(1 - e\u00b2/c\u00b2) = (a\u00b2 - c\u00b2)( (c\u00b2 - e\u00b2)/c\u00b2 )\n\nSimilarly, from the hyperbola:\n\ny\u00b2 = b_h\u00b2(x\u00b2/e\u00b2 - 1) = (c\u00b2 - e\u00b2)( (a\u00b2e\u00b2)/(c\u00b2e\u00b2) - 1 ) = (c\u00b2 - e\u00b2)( a\u00b2/c\u00b2 - 1 ) = (c\u00b2 - e\u00b2)( (a\u00b2 - c\u00b2)/c\u00b2 )\n\nNotice that both expressions for y\u00b2 are the same:\n\nFrom ellipse: (a\u00b2 - c\u00b2)(c\u00b2 - e\u00b2)/c\u00b2\n\nFrom hyperbola: (c\u00b2 - e\u00b2)(a\u00b2 - c\u00b2)/c\u00b2\n\nSo, y\u00b2 = (a\u00b2 - c\u00b2)(c\u00b2 - e\u00b2)/c\u00b2\n\nTherefore, y = \u00b1 sqrt[(a\u00b2 - c\u00b2)(c\u00b2 - e\u00b2)] / c\n\nBut maybe we don't need the exact value of y. Let's 