an be negative or positive. However, if cos\u2077(15x) is negative, then the product cos\u2077(15x)*cos\u00b2(9x) is negative, so subtracting a negative number would make the left-hand side larger than 1. But 1 - (negative number) is greater than 1, so the fourth root is defined and real, and equals sin(9x), which is non-negative. But the right-hand side is sin(9x), which is between 0 and 1 (since sin(9x) \u2265 0). So even if the left-hand side is greater than 1, the fourth root would be greater than 1, but sin(9x) can only be up to 1. Therefore, perhaps there's a contradiction when cos\u2077(15x) is negative. Let's check that.\n\nIf cos(15x) is negative, then cos\u2077(15x) is negative (since 7 is odd), so the product cos\u2077(15x)*cos\u00b2(9x) is negative. Then 1 - (negative number) is 1 + |cos\u2077(15x)*cos\u00b2(9x)|, which is greater than 1. Therefore, the left-hand side of the equation (the fourth root) is equal to [1 + |cos\u2077(15x)*cos\u00b2(9x)|]^(1/4), which is greater than 1^(1/4) = 1. However, the right-hand side is sin(9x), which is at most 1. Therefore, in such cases, the equation cannot hold because the left-hand side would be greater than 1, and the right-hand side is at most 1. Hence, the only possible solutions occur when cos\u2077(15x)*cos\u00b2(9x) is non-negative, which would require that cos\u2077(15x) is non-negative. Since cos\u00b2(9x) is always non-negative, the product is non-negative only if cos\u2077(15x) is non-negative. Therefore, cos(15x) \u2265 0, because any even power would make it positive, but here it's raised to the 7th power, which preserves the sign. Therefore, cos(15x) \u2265 0.\n\nTherefore, we can restrict our solutions to those x where cos(15x) \u2265 0 and sin(9x) \u2265 0.\n\nSo, now we have two conditions:\n\n1. sin(9x) \u2265 0 => 9x \u2208 [2\u03c0k, \u03c0 + 2\u03c0k] for some integer k => x \u2208 [2\u03c0k/9, \u03c0/9 + 2\u03c0k/9]\n\n2. cos(15x) \u2265 0 => 15x \u2208 [-\u03c0/2 + 2\u03c0n, \u03c0/2 + 2\u03c