des to the fourth power to eliminate the fourth root:\n\n\\[\n1 - \\cos^7(15x) \\cos^2(9x) = \\sin^4(9x)\n\\]\n\nWe analyze the domain conditions: \\(\\sin(9x) \\geq 0\\) and \\(1 - \\cos^7(15x) \\cos^2(9x) \\geq 0\\). This implies \\(\\cos(15x) \\geq 0\\) when \\(\\cos^2(9x) \\neq 0\\).\n\n### Case 1: \\(\\sin(9x) = 0\\)\nThis implies \\(9x = \\pi k\\) for integers \\(k\\), so \\(x = \\frac{\\pi k}{9}\\). For the equation to hold, we need \\(\\cos^7(15x) \\cos^2(9x) = 1\\). This requires \\(\\cos(15x) = 1\\) and \\(\\cos(9x) = \\pm 1\\). Solving these, we find \\(x = \\frac{2\\pi m}{3}\\) for integers \\(m\\).\n\n### Case 2: \\(\\sin(9x) > 0\\)\nHere, we consider \\(\\cos(9x) = 0\\), which implies \\(9x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\pi k\\), leading to \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{18} + \\frac{\\pi k}{9}\\). Checking these solutions, we find they satisfy the original equation.\n\n### Combining Solutions\nThe solutions from both cases are combined, considering the periodicity of the trigonometric functions involved. The solutions are:\n\n1. \\(x = \\frac{2\\pi m}{3}\\) for integers \\(m\\)\n2. \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{18} + \\frac{2\\pi k}{9}\\) for integers \\(k\\)\n\nThus, the final solutions are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x = \\frac{\\pi}{3} k} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\boxed{x = \\frac{\\pi}{18} + \\frac{2\\pi}{9} k} \\quad \\text{for any integer } k.\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo solve the equation:\n\n\\[ \\sqrt[4]{1-\\cos ^{7}(15x) \\cos ^{2}(9x)} = \\sin(9x) \\]\n\nwe assume the condition \\( \\sin(9x) \\geq 0 \\), enabling us to raise both sides of this equation to the fourth power:\n\n\\[ \\left( \\sqrt[4]{1-\\cos ^{7}(15x) \\cos ^{2}(9x)}\\right)^4 = (\\sin(9x))^4 \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[ 1 - \\cos ^{7}(15x) \\cos ^{2}(9x) = \\sin ^{4}(9x) \\]\n\nNext, recalling the Pythagorean identity \\( \\sin^2(9x) + \\cos^2(9x) = 1 \\), we have:\n\n\\[ \\sin^2(9x) = 1 - \\cos^2(9x) \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\[ \\sin^4(9x) = \\left(1 - \\cos^2(9x)\\right)^2 \\]\n\nSubstituting this ba