ascending order:\n   \\[\n   0, 1, 3, 5, 8\n   \\]\n\n3. **Identify the Middle Number:**\n   - With 5 numbers, the middle number is the 3rd number in the ordered list (since it will have two numbers on either side).\n   - In the list \\( 0, 1, 3, 5, 8 \\), the 3rd number is \\( 3 \\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   Therefore, when the numbers are arranged from smallest to largest, the middle number is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{3}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Gavin has a collection of 50 songs that are each 3 minutes in length and 50 songs that are each 5 minutes in length. What is the maximum number of songs from his collection that he can play in 3 hours? \n\n(A) 100  \n(B) 36  \n(C) 56  \n(D) 60  \n(E) 45", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem step by step. So, Gavin has 50 songs that are 3 minutes each and another 50 songs that are 5 minutes each. He wants to play as many songs as possible within 3 hours. The options are given, and we need to find the maximum number of songs he can play. Hmm, let's start by understanding the total time available.\n\nFirst, convert 3 hours into minutes because the song lengths are given in minutes. 3 hours is 3 * 60 = 180 minutes. Got that. So, the total time he has is 180 minutes.\n\nNow, he wants to maximize the number of songs. Since shorter songs take less time, playing more 3-minute songs would allow him to fit more songs into the 180 minutes. But he only has 50 of those. Similarly, the 5-minute songs are longer, so they take more time, but he also has 50 of those. So, the strategy here is probably to play as many 3-minute songs as possible first, and then fill the remaining time with 5-minute songs. Let me check if that's the case.\n\nIf he plays all 50 of the 3-minute songs, that would take 50 * 3 = 150 minutes. Then, the remaining time would be 180 - 150 = 30 minutes. With 30 minutes left, he can play 30 / 5 = 6 songs of 5 mi